🧱 STRUCTURE: ArrayList vs LinkedList
🧩 PATTERN: Two Pointers (array/list thinking)

execution → memory → when to use → how to explain in interview.

PART 1️⃣: What they REALLY are
❌ Wrong way (too shallow)
ArrayList → dynamic array
LinkedList → doubly linked list

✅ Correct mental model (THIS matters)
🟦 ArrayList
    ArrayList = continuous memory + index-based access
    Think:
    [ A ][ B ][ C ][ D ][ E ]
     0    1    2    3    4
    Stored contiguously
    Direct access using index
    Resizes when full

🟨 LinkedList
    LinkedList = nodes connected by pointers
    Think:
    null <- [A] <-> [B] <-> [C] <-> [D] -> null
    Each node:
    prev | data | next
    Not stored contiguously
    Access only by traversal
    Extra memory for pointers

PART 2️⃣: EXECUTION MODEL (this is where confusion ends)
🔍 Accessing element
ArrayList
    list.get(5);
    What happens:
    Jump directly to memory index 5
    ✅ O(1)
    implements RandomAccess markup interface
    Better for Retrieval

LinkedList
    list.get(5);
    What happens:
    Start from head
    Traverse node by node
    ❌ O(n)
    not implements RandomAccess markup interface
    Better for insertion/Deletion with reference (not with index, not for retrieval)

🧠 IMPORTANT INTERVIEW POINT
LinkedList does NOT give O(1) access by index.

PART 3️⃣: ADD operation (this is where people get fooled)
Case 1: Add at END
ArrayList
    list.add(x);
    If space exists → O(1)
    If resize happens → O(n) (copy)
    Amortized O(1)

LinkedList
    list.add(x);
    Add node, update pointers
    O(1)

➡️ Both are good at add-at-end

Case 2: Add at MIDDLE
ArrayList
    list.add(2, x);
    What happens:
    Shift elements right
    ❌ O(n)

LinkedList
    list.add(2, x);
    What happens:
    Traverse to index 2 → O(n)
    Insert node → O(1)
    ➡️ Still O(n) overall

🚨 BIG TRUTH
LinkedList is NOT faster for insert in middle unless you already have the node reference.

PART 4️⃣: REMOVE operation (same trap)
Remove by index
ArrayList
    Shift elements
    ❌ O(n)

LinkedList
    Traverse to node
    ❌ O(n)

Remove by reference (Iterator)
iterator.remove();
ArrayList → O(n)
LinkedList → O(1)

🧠 This is the ONLY real advantage of LinkedList

PART 5️⃣: MEMORY (interviewers LOVE this)
ArrayList
    Stores only data
    Less memory
    Cache-friendly

LinkedList
    Stores:
    data
    prev pointer
    next pointer
    ❌ more memory
    ❌ poor cache locality
    ➡️ LinkedList is heavier

PART 6️⃣: Time Complexity (clear table)
Operation	    ArrayList	LinkedList
get(i)	        O(1)	    O(n)
add(end)	    O(1)*	    O(1)
add(middle)	    O(n)	    O(n)
remove(end)	    O(1)	    O(1)
remove(middle)	O(n)	    O(n)
memory	        low	        high

* amortized (if array full then need to copy all element in new array and insert at end O(n))

PART 7️⃣: WHEN TO USE WHAT (THIS IS CRITICAL)
✅ Use ArrayList when:
    Frequent access (get)
    Iteration
    Memory matters
    Most applications

❌ Do NOT use LinkedList just because:
“Insertion is faster” ❌ (myth)
✅ Use LinkedList ONLY when:
    You frequently insert/remove via iterator
    You are implementing Queue / Deque
    You need constant-time head/tail ops

PART 8️⃣: Interview-ready explanation (MEMORIZE - This answer sounds senior-level.)
“ArrayList provides fast random access because elements are stored contiguously, while LinkedList requires traversal.
Although LinkedList allows O(1) insertion with a node reference, most operations still take O(n).
In practice, ArrayList is preferred unless frequent iterator-based insertions or deletions are required.”

PART 9️⃣: PATTERN — Two Pointers (Array/List thinking)
This pattern pairs naturally with ArrayList/array.

Example: Reverse list
int i = 0, j = list.size() - 1;
while (i < j) {
    Collections.swap(list, i, j);
    i++; j--;
}

Why NOT LinkedList?
No index access
Two pointers inefficient
