2️⃣ One case where LinkedList is actually better
This is where we need a precise correction.
You said:
“when we have to insert or remove in middle LinkedList is best”
❌ This is the common myth
LinkedList is NOT automatically better for middle insert/remove.

✅ The correct condition (VERY IMPORTANT)
LinkedList is better only when you already have the node reference
(for example, via an Iterator).

✅ Correct interview-level answer
“LinkedList is better when frequent insertions or deletions are performed using an iterator or when working at the head or tail,
because those operations are O(1) once the node reference is available.”

🧠 Why your original statement is partially wrong (execution view)
Insert at middle by index:
list.add(5, x);
ArrayList:
Direct index access → shift elements → O(n)

LinkedList:
Traverse to index 5 → O(n)
Insert node → O(1)

➡️ Overall still O(n) for LinkedList
So LinkedList is not faster here.

Insert using Iterator (this is the real advantage)
ListIterator<Integer> it = list.listIterator();
while (it.hasNext()) {
    if (it.next() == 5) {
        it.add(100);   // O(1) in LinkedList
    }
}
LinkedList → O(1) insertion
ArrayList → O(n) (shift elements)
✅ THIS is the correct use case.

🔑 Final mental rule (lock this in)
LinkedList is good when you already know WHERE you are (iterator).
ArrayList is good when you need to JUMP (index).

