📌 Where Stack & Vector fit (big picture)
Iterable
 └── Collection
      └── List
           ├── ArrayList   ✅ (done)
           ├── LinkedList  ✅ (done)
           ├── Vector      ⚠️
           └── Stack       ⚠️ (legacy)

Stack extends Vector
Vector extends AbstractList
This inheritance is the ROOT of confusion.

🧱 STRUCTURE 3: Vector (Execution + Why it exists)
What Vector REALLY is
Vector is a synchronized ArrayList
Internally:
Dynamic array (like ArrayList)
Index-based access
BUT all methods are synchronized

Execution difference: Vector vs ArrayList
ArrayList
list.add(x);   // NOT thread-safe

Vector
vector.add(x); // thread-safe (synchronized)

Why this matters
Synchronization = locking
Locking = slower

🧠 Why Vector is mostly avoided today
Reason	                Explanation
Performance	            Synchronization on every method
Coarse locking	        Entire object locked
Legacy	                Introduced in Java 1.0
Better alternatives	    Collections.synchronizedList, CopyOnWriteArrayList

Interview sentence:
“Vector is thread-safe but inefficient due to coarse-grained synchronization, so it’s rarely used today.”

When Vector MAY be used (rare)
Very old legacy code
Simple thread-safe list without performance concern

