🧱 STRUCTURE 4: Stack (THIS IS IMPORTANT)
What Stack REALLY is
Stack is a legacy class that extends Vector and represents LIFO behavior
That’s already a design smell.

Stack behavior (LIFO)
Stack<Integer> s = new Stack<>();
s.push(1);
s.push(2);
s.push(3);

s.pop(); // 3
s.peek(); // 2

🔥 WHY Stack is considered BAD DESIGN
Problem 1: Wrong inheritance
Stack is-a Vector ❌
But conceptually:
Stack ≠ List
Stack should NOT expose add(index) etc.

You can do this:
stack.add(0, 99); // breaks stack discipline
❌ That violates stack rules.

Problem 2: Thread safety overhead
Because Stack extends Vector:
All methods are synchronized
Performance hit

✅ What should be used instead of Stack?
👉 Deque (ArrayDeque)
Deque<Integer> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
stack.push(1);
stack.push(2);
stack.pop();

Why Deque is better
No unnecessary synchronization
Clean LIFO semantics
Faster

🧠 Interview-ready explanation for Stack
“Stack is a legacy class that extends Vector and provides LIFO operations, but it’s generally discouraged because it exposes unnecessary List methods
and has synchronization overhead. Deque implementations like ArrayDeque are preferred.”

🧠 Comparison summary (LOCK THIS TABLE)
Structure	    Thread-safe	    Access	        Use today
ArrayList	    ❌	            O(1) index	    ✅ YES
LinkedList	    ❌	            O(n)	        ⚠️ Rare
Vector	        ✅	            O(1) index	    ❌ Legacy
Stack	        ✅	            LIFO	        ❌ Legacy
ArrayDeque	    ❌	            LIFO/FIFO	    ✅ YES

🔑 VERY IMPORTANT CONCEPT (this removes future confusion)
Java legacy collections tried to mix data structure + thread safety.
Modern Java separates them.
That’s why:
Stack → deprecated in spirit
Vector → avoided
Deque → preferred

